    "narrative": "For the July 21 slate, the Zurich Zebras lean heavily on high-ceiling power-speed combinations, headlined by **Yordan Alvarez** (82.97) and **Trea Turner** (81.27). **Yordan Alvarez** remains the premier play against Tyler Phillips, bolstered by a massive 0.473 xwOBA projection and a +19% boost from the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill profile. Meanwhile, **Trea Turner** is locked into the leadoff spot against the southpaw Justin Wrobleski; with an 85% stolen base success rate against the pitcher's slow delivery and a favorable wind-out condition, **Trea Turner** is our primary engine for both runs and the scarcity-driven SB category. **Kyle Schwarber** (76.62) rounds out our elite tier, taking advantage of similar favorable winds to capitalize on his dynamic platoon edge against the lefty Wrobleski.\n\nWhile the top-tier plays are secure, we are monitoring significant volatility at the bottom of the lineup. **Dillon Dingler** (57.51) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (58.43) both carry a 41% rain risk in their respective matchups, with wind-in conditions suppressing their scoring upside. **Carson Benge** was the most difficult exclusion from the starting nine; despite a solid 56.34 score, he was ultimately edged out by **Nelson Velazquez** (56.53), who benefits from a more favorable hitting environment and superior SP skill matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a. Elsewhere, **Casey Schmitt** (63.92) and **Junior Caminero** (64.81) provide stability in the middle infield and third base slots, both benefiting from dynamic platoon advantages that elevate them comfortably above our 60-point \"Strong\" threshold. We remain cautious regarding **Hyeseong Kim**, who continues to stagnate in the minors, and we will keep a close eye on official lineup postings to ensure that our assumed batting orders for **Gary Sanchez** and others hold true, as any shift down the order could necessitate a re-evaluation of our efficiency scores.",
